out the guy Daniel  gave him $20 and our son said he would  find a way to get him some pot it took  the snodgrass his son three weeks to buy  half a joint of pot off a homeless man  this process was repeated a second time  when Daniel asked a third time their son  refused after that Daniel stopped  communicating with him altogether which  our son seems pretty broken up about and  he was back to back to zero friends so  he was alone again  and it really hurt him a few weeks later  armed policeman walked into his  classroom and arrested him in front of  his peers his parents were not notified  of the arrest at the time he was taken  to a juvenile facility for 48 hours 22  students were arrested in the drugs  most of them were special needs students  causing police tactics to come into  question our current policy for dealing  with this stuff is is a complete failure  we need to rethink our policy all we're  doing is using the hammer and we're  creating new nails like these children  here Stephen downing spent 20 years on  the force with the Los Angeles Police  Department located two counties  northwest of Riverside the LAPD  pioneered drug busts in high schools in  1974 the Los Angeles School District  General Council launched a review into  the undercover program in 2004 the  review found no evidence that the  program reduced the amount of drugs on  school grounds however there was  evidence of an increase in arrests of  special education students the LAPD  disbanded the program in 2005 after the  review concluded that had failed to  catch serious drug offenders in this  case there was no crime until the kid  had been talked into going out on the  street and finding something and he did  it to keep his friendship for somebody  that's a sting that's a nasty degrading  sting a Department of Justice report  likewise concluded that sting operations  in general do nothing to solve long-term  crime and that they may prevent the use  of other more effective problem-solving 